  "strategy": "The central idea is to use a bilevel optimization approach that combines multi-start gradient-based optimization with a novel step function construction based on random Fourier features for the interval [0, 2]. This approach aims to surpass the current record by exploring the function space more effectively and ensuring strict feasibility through integral projection. The algorithm uses smooth approximations to handle non-differentiable max operations, and incorporates resolution changes to balance between local refinement and global exploration.\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The algorithm uses random Fourier features to create step functions with potentially lower correlation peaks. By projecting each candidate to maintain integral 1.0, we ensure strict feasibility. The bilevel design allows for both local optimization (SLSQP) and global design (random Fourier fields). The regularization parameters in the smooth max objective control how aggressively the algorithm avoids self-overlap while staying within bounds.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Objective: \n   minimize C5 = max_{k} \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   Subject to:\n     n_point values h[i] \u2208 [0,1] with n = 540\n     \u2211(h[i] * dx) = 1.0 (where dx=2/n)\n\n   Optimization:\n   Outer loop: Sample from chi-squared distribution (smooth step function)\n   Inner loop: Use SLSQP with projected gradient from the smooth relaxation\n\n   Feasibility:\n     Precompute random feature weights \u03c9_\u03c6\n     Project samples using: h_proj = (\u03c7/\u03c7_max) * \u03c1_clip(\u03c6\u00b7\u03be_\u03c7 + b)\n     Adjust by scaling to satisfy integral constraint\n\n   Smooth relaxation:\n     F_\u03b1(h) = \u03b1\u00b7log(1 + exp( (-\u03ba_max) / \u03b1)) + \n               (1/\u03b1)\u00b7\u2211_{l} exp(\u03b1\u00b7(C(l) - M\u0302))\n     Where M\u0302 is scale-normalized max time series\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Initialize using parent solution if available\n   - Select 100 basis features for smooth random step functions\n   - For each \u03c6 sample:\n        \u2022 Generate candidate using \u03c7 = \u03c7_max\u00b7tukey(two_tailed=0.8)\n        \u2022 Project to valid interval and sum constraint\n        \u2022 Use SLSQP with projected gradient and random restarts\n        \u2022 Track best candidate with lowest C5\n   - Retrain features adaptively for higher resolutions\n\n   SLSQP Configuration:\n     Max iterations: min(300, sigma\u00b7floor(1300/time_budget_seconds))\n     Initial trust region: 0.02\n     Integral constraint handling:\n        \u2211(h[i]*dx) = 1.0\n        Implemented via projection per step\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Primary variation: Regularization by (1 - h_factor) versus (h_factor)\n   - Resolution adaptation: \n        Levels: 1080, 2160, 4320\n        Triggered by wall clock (time \u2264 1300 seconds)\n        Use upsampling with integral normalization\n   - Temperature schedule for gradient noise:\n        \u03c1_t = exp(-iterations/(5000*\u03c4))\n        Used in smooth relaxation\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Primary time sink: \n        Feature generation: O(100) per evaluation\n        Correlation: O(2n-1) ~ O(n)\n        SLSQP steps: O(n^2 + n) per function call\n   - Memory profiling: Keep only shortest history (last 10 candidates)\n   - Time allocation:\n        \u2022 Main optimization: 70%\n        \u2022 Rescue procedures: 20%\n        \u2022 Resolution boost: 10%\n\n   Fallback procedure:\n        If all fails, revert to projected parent solution\n        Reuse grid points in perturbation\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Execute final candidate against original Erd\u0151s objective\n   Perform:\n     \u2022 Feasibility check: \u2211(h*dx) - 1.0\n     \u2022 Non-negativity: 1 - \u2211(h[i]*10^-12)\n     \u2022 Compute C5 using numpy.correlate(maxlag=None)\n   Return with methodology: \"Bilevel Fourier Random Feature\"",
